WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.780
[gentle music]

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.320
Chiplets are revolutionizing
semiconductor design by enabling

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.080
flexible modular architectures
that improve scalability,

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.780
performance, and cost efficiency.

5
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.980
For decades, semiconductor advancements
relied on monolithic chips,

6
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.280
single large integrated circuits
packed with billions of transistors.

7
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.320
However, as computing demands grow,

8
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.580
these chips are approaching
physical and economic limitations.

9
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.600
Higher complexity leads
to increased power consumption,

10
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.900
manufacturing defects, and lower yields.

11
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.920
The industry now requires a more adaptable
and scalable approach to chip design.

12
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.860
Chiplets provide an alternative
approach by breaking down

13
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.620
a larger chip into smaller
modular components.

14
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.270
These chiplets can be manufactured

15
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.140
separately and assembled
together in a package.

16
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.710
Heterogeneous integration
builds on this concept by

17
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.360
allowing chiplets from different
process nodes, functions,

18
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.480
or even different manufacturers
to work together seamlessly.

19
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.340
This approach enables optimization.

20
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.540
Compute chips can be built
on leading-edge nodes,

21
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.980
while analog and memory components
can use mature, cost-effective nodes.

22
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.540
There are multiple methods
for integrating chiplets,

23
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.240
including 2D, 2.5D, and 3D stacking,

24
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.000
each offering trade-offs
in performance, power, and cost.

25
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.860
Technological advances make it possible
to go both horizontal and vertical.

26
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.500
With two-dimensional packages,

27
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.060
multiple dies can be mounted on
a package substrate that connects them.

28
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.270
In 2.5D, packaging enables dies to be

29
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.260
mounted on silicon interposer
and interconnected with finer metal wires.

30
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.940
In 3D, multiple dies can be stacked
or mounted on top of other dies,

31
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.379
enabling even more and shorter
connections between the dies.

32
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.280
In many cases, 2D, 2.5D,

33
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.760
and 3D may be mixed in the same package,

34
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.580
giving more flexibility for
heterogeneous integration.

35
00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.480
With chiplet-based silicon design,
common functions like core, cache,

36
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.730
memory, and I/O can be sliced into modular

37
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.420
dies and packaged together
to form a larger system.

38
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.600
Today, silicon area can be
1700 millimeters squared,

39
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.740
nearly double the 850 millimeter
squared size of monolithic dies.

40
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.380
And what's more, package sizes are
projected to double in the next few years,

41
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.180
opening up significant
growth opportunities.

42
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.760
[gentle music]

